meta {
  name: Update User
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/{{userId}}
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Cookie: auth_token={{authToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "first_name": "Updated",
    "middle_name": "",
    "last_name": "Name",
    "email": "updated@example.com",
    "mobile": "9876543210",
    "role_name": "senior_sales_executive",
    "manager_id": null,
    "is_active": true,
    "force_password_change": false,
    "send_email_notification": true
  }
}

docs {
  last_working_day (optional, YYYY-MM-DD) and exit_reason (optional, max 100): only with
  is_active=false. HR records them as the profile's exit date and reason. Omitted date =
  today unless HR already set one; reactivating (is_active=true) clears the exit date.

  send_email_notification (optional, default false: opt-in, nothing is sent unless true): only acted on when this PATCH
  actually changes the user's branch access or home branch (a plain profile edit
  sends nothing). When true AND the acting admin holds admin.team.notify (or rank
  >= org_admin), the user is emailed which branches were added/removed and the
  new home branch. Set false to skip.

  email, when supplied, is normalized to lowercase and trimmed before it is
  stored (schema 1.48.0) -- the same rule as create. Editing a user's email to a
  mixed-case value lands the canonical spelling; the database refuses anything
  else (chk_users_email_lowercase).

  Response: 200 { success: true, data: { hr_profile_synced } } (was 204). Every
  update re-syncs the member's hr.employee_profiles row (home branch + is_active)
  through hr-service; hr_profile_synced false means the identity change is saved
  but the HR profile is not yet in step.

  manager_id: omit it to leave the reporting line untouched. Sending it (even
  the same value) supersedes today's line; null closes it, which also removes
  the user's leave approver chain.

  Deactivating ("is_active": false) hands over the user's leads in EVERY branch
  they hold, not only home:
    reassign_leads_to        home branch. uuid = new owner, null OR omitted =
                             unassign (a deactivation never leaves leads on the
                             account; omitted still means "leave them" for a
                             branch MOVE).
    reassign_leads_by_org    { "<org_id>": "<user uuid>" | null } for each OTHER
                             branch. A branch left out is unassigned. Only valid
                             together with is_active:false; a key that is the
                             home branch, or not one of the user's branches, is a
                             400. A uuid must be an active, lead-working member
                             of THAT branch and the actor must manage it.
  reassign_leads_by_org is also accepted together with org_assignments: every
  branch the new set drops (other than the old home a move is leaving) has its
  leads handed over / unassigned before the mapping is deactivated.
  Leads are handed over before the account is deactivated, so a failure leaves
  the user active and the request can simply be retried.
}
